ock to address  climate change  and and doing so i'd i'm going to bring  in a few case studies that really help  emphasize just how potent this  technology can be  um i also hope to draw in the uh  synergism that can exist between  breeding and gene editing and conclude  with some final remarks  so  as you've all probably are well aware of  climate change stands to impact the food  system quite negatively  and the environmental outcomes of  climate change are wide-ranging and  quite severe  climate change might facilitate  increased salinization  enhanced droughts  increase the concentration of co2  temperatures these are  statistics and phenomena that have been  looming for a while and that we have  been aware of  and i just want to emphasize here how  devastating climate change is and will  continue to be in terms of agricultural  systems some of the impacts that we can  anticipate are increased disease  severity as a result of expanding the  host ranges of a lot of pathogens or  disease-causing agents  decreasing the ability of crops and  livestock to manage their resistance  disease among a plethora of other  impacts heightening the disease severity  in crops and livestock  projections  more or less always converge on an  understanding that yields whether vegan  crops or in livestock will be lowered as  a result of the impacts of climate  change and we can also anticipate lower  nutritive quality primarily of our crops  so collectively we know that climate  change will negatively impact our food  systems and  this figure this understanding of how  climate change will impact our food  systems is not necessarily  all-encompassing but meant to highlight  kind of the most poignant impacts that  we can anticipate  so with this bleak understanding of food  systems in the face of climate change i  hope to create a little bit of hope for  us and understanding what can we do  about it specifically in the lens of  gene editing  and so  the work that has uh surfaced to address  climate change and agriculture has  converged into four major traits and  those are disease mitigation enhanced  yields abiotic stress tolerance and  nutritional improvement and all of these  traits our phenotypes stand to be  affected by climate change um and so  it's very hopeful or promising to see  that there has been a large body of work  in embracing the realities of climate  change and being able to address them  quite effectively through the  applications of gene editing  and so  i want to share a bit about what that  work has looked like on a temporal scale  so there's been a really recent surge of  innovations that have been facilitated  by gene editing and you can see here in  both crops and livestock  this  figure is skewed left so there's been a  primarily a large surge of innovations  that have happened recently  especially if you look at the year 2018  for example and this is largely this  recent surge in innovation i believe can  be largely attributed to the uh  innovation of crispr cas9 editing and so  the first eukaryotic cells were edited  in 2013 and regenerated plants were  occurred for the first time in 2015. and  so you can see that crispr cast 9 and  the advent of  ability to use crispr cast 9 in vivo has  really facilitated a recent surge of  innovations which is exciting and  creates a lot of promise for how gene  editing innovation will continue to  merge and continue to be a potent  mechanism to address the emerging and  current uh  impositions of climate change  and so beyond temporal scale uh  understanding that gene editing can be  applied widely uh biologically so you  see here separated in green for crops  and purple for livestock the range of  organisms that have successfully been  gene targeted i mean there is an  expansive set now organisms that have  had this technology successfully applied  to them and you can see that  these organisms range from staple crops  like wheat and maize and rice all the  way to more  locally imported crops like ground  cherries and so this is a really  exciting understanding that this  technology can be expansive for  global staple crops um local populations  and um  among a variety of traits and you can  see the same is true in livestock there  are quite severe bottlenecks in  livestock editing  in terms of regeneration and tissue  culture that are need to be surmounted  in order to expand the range of  organisms  livestock organisms that can be targeted  for improvement with gene editing but  this figure is meant to establish an  understanding that  gene editing can indeed be applied to a  wide variety of organisms and this  enhances the potency that gene editing  technology  stands to offer  and so  what is that potency what has this  technology achieved  you can see here a gallery of figures of  gene edited organisms all for different  traits and so  everything highlighted in orange is a  yield improvement trait in blue is  nutrition quality improvement trait  red and abiotic stress tolerance trade  and in green is a  disease resistance trait and so  i think let's start with the orange  because they're the most visually  striking in my opinion you can see a red  sea bream on the left one that's been  edited at a single locus the mstn gene  which is a locus myostatin gene thought  to be able to increase muscle mass and  based on visual inspection i'd say that  is indeed the case a red sebring that  had been edited accumulated 16  skeletal muscle on average um below that  you can see that same locus being  targeted in cattle and the cow on the  right is a little bit more muscular than  the one on the left  um  alternatively you see that yield  improvements have also been achieved in  crops  in this case tomato promoter editing of  important uh tomato inflorescence and  developmental genes  yielded fruit with increased  locul numbers and fruit size and so i  think that's a very striking image of  how gene editing could be applied to  enhance yield  what's more is in nutrition you can see  the beta carotene accumulation in this  banana that has been facilitated by a  knockout of a single gene  in chickens you can reduce the amount of  fat accumulation on the gi tract  which will be an important mechanism for  lowering the negative food quality  attributes present in livestock  you see on the bottom left in rice a  gene editing approach that enhances  saline resistance um  like i mentioned previously climate  change is anticipated to increase the  salinization and so  you can see uh that plant on the left  that's wild type and on the right are  rice plants that have been edited for a  single gene and they are  growing larger by far on average  and then finally we get to disease  resistance and crops and lifestyle so  the pigs that you see  are ones that have been gene edited  successfully and they are resistant to  um  horstene respiratory virus and so when  challenged with the virus these pigs are  the only ones that survive and their  counterparts their wild type  counterparts actually need to be  euthanized because the severity of the  disease  and finally you see on the right um  field trials of gene edited  rice plants for broad spectrum  resistance in this case they're  challenged with rice blast in the field  and you can see that the rice on the  right looks a lot healthier and it will  yield on average 50 percent more per  hectare based on  having a single gene edit a single locus  i think at this point given the data  shared it's undeniable that gene editing  is an incredibly powerful technique and  technology to facilitate improvements in  crops and livestock alike for uh  addressing climate change and addressing  performance issues beyond the  impositions of climate change um and so  this is an incredibly exciting  technology but it by no means does it  forego  or lessen the importance of conventional  breeding techniques that have existed  before gene editing and will continue to  persist beyond it as you 